-{#
-  Deploy-window tolerance (2026-07-26): a routine mining-pool deploy restarts
-  the stratum coordinator that serves the docker-proxied PRISM metrics
-  endpoint, and the 2026-07-26 mainnet #98 rollout showed the resulting
-  scrape gap (~8s port window + 15s scrape interval + 1m evaluation lag)
-  paging DatasourceNoData from any coordinator-scrape rule that treats
-  NoData as alertable. Every rule below therefore declares an explicit
-  no_data_state/exec_err_state. Coordinator-scrape threshold rules keep
-  NoData/ExecErr = OK and rely on the dedicated coverage nets — one
-  Alerting-on-NoData net per scrape surface (PrismMetricsNoData and
-  PrismMetricsEndpointDown for the coordinator endpoint,
-  PrismPublicLedgerUnready for the extracted read tier,
-  PrismStratumHealthNoData for the sidecar, PrismBackupMetricsNoData for
-  the backup collector) — so a planned restart resolves to OK without ever
-  hiding a real outage, and those nets plus the coordinator threshold rules
-  carry for: >= 3m to outlast a planned restart. The stratum-health sidecar
-  probe rules are the deploy-independent real-outage net and deliberately
-  keep their tighter 1m/2m windows (probe patience during lane restarts was
-  fixed separately via #648). Severity=info rules stay routed to Slack
-  (grouped under alert_class=prism-runtime): after the 2026-07-26 threshold
-  fixes they only fire on sustained ledger failures or post-restart replay,
-  both worth surfacing, and only critical page=true rules escalate to
-  GoAlert. The backup staleness criticals detect an operation series that
-  never succeeded through a collector-gated guard (collector series
-  present, operation series absent) rather than NoData=Alerting, so a
-  transient node-exporter scrape gap stays behind PrismBackupMetricsNoData's
-  five-minute grace instead of bursting immediate criticals (2026-07-26).
-  Where a guard shares an expression with an aggregated bool branch (WAL,
-  audit, coverage), every branch is label_replace-tagged with a distinct
-  label value inside the max(): a bare "max(...) or guard" lets a healthy
-  0-valued max() result shadow the guard in the PromQL union -- both
-  collapse to the empty label signature and "or" keeps the left side -- so
-  the missing-series critical would silently never fire (2026-07-29).
-  Absence-guard tolerance (2026-07-31): at 15:17:53Z a backup-metrics
-  collector run overlapped the qbit-prism-audit-backup run (93.9GB in ~31s,
-  every 10 minutes), one probe hit its --timeout 5, and the collector
-  published its textfile without the full/diff operation series. The guards
-  did exactly what they were built to do and PrismBackupFullStaleCritical,
-  PrismBackupDifferentialStaleCritical and PrismBackupCollectorStale all
-  fired at 15:18Z against healthy backups. The immediate-guard design above
-  stays: a truly-missing series must never hide behind NoData grace, so the
-  fix is a short pending window rather than NoData=Alerting or a weaker
-  guard. Every rule whose expression carries a collector-gated "unless"
-  branch now uses qbit_monitoring_stack_prism_backup_alert_absence_guard_for
-  (3m) instead of the shared immediate "for", so a single ~60s collector
-  omission window can no longer page while a series that never succeeded
-  still alerts within minutes. The age-based halves of those expressions are
-  continuous, so +3m is immaterial against 10-day and 48-hour thresholds.
-  PrismBackupCollectorStale deliberately keeps the immediate "for": it is
-  the intended early, non-paging Slack signal that the collector itself is
-  degraded.
-  Chronic sidecar failure visibility (2026-07-31): PrismStratumUnavailable
-  is a min()-over-lanes outage net gated on
-  qbit_monitoring_stack_prism_alert_stratum_down_for, so a lane that fails a
-  probe and recovers inside that window never notifies: 877 prism and 273
-  prism-highdiff sidecar check failures in a single day surfaced as only
-  three pages. The sidecar
-  now exports a cumulative qbit_stratum_health_check_failures_total counter
-  (its qbit_stratum_health_check_failures gauge only holds the consecutive
-  streak and resets on every recovery, so chronic flapping is invisible to
-  increase()), and PrismStratumCheckFailureRateHigh reports a sustained
-  failure rate as a non-paging severity=warning Slack signal. The paging
-  contract is unchanged.
-  Overload contract (2026-08-24): bootstrap #191/#188 makes stale complete
-  metric snapshots scrapeable as HTTP 200, exports the exact unresolved-parent
-  depth/cap, and specifies the landing, backlog, semantic-coverage, refresh,
-  and snapshot alerts rendered below. Bootstrap #199 adds the direct
-  accepted-block-to-preview histogram and its healthy/degraded result split.
-  They stay behind an explicit pin gate: a pre-#184 build returns 503 for stale
-  snapshots and cannot support the body rules honestly. The post-#199 rules
-  are safe across a fast rollback to #196 because absent histogram series are
-  explicitly OK. PrismMetricsEndpointDown is this deployment's PrismScrapeDown
-  equivalent and fires after the existing deploy-safe three-minute window.
-  Paging audit (2026-09-09, qbit-tools #1086): the coverage watchdogs
-  (PrismMetricsEndpointDown, PrismStratumHealthNoData,
-  PrismPublicLedgerUnready, PrismMetricsSnapshotUnavailable) fire on
-  monitoring blindness rather than miner impact -- 61 of
-  PrismMetricsEndpointDown's 63 fires in 45 days duplicated the Slack-only
-  PrismMetricsNoData and 31 duplicated PrismStratumUnavailable -- so they
-  are severity=warning Slack signals with no page label. The miner-facing
-  outage pages through PrismStratumUnavailable and host loss through
-  QbitNodeExporterDown.
-  Writer-lease headroom (2026-08-31): bootstrap #214 exports its validated
-  policy terms beside server-proven age and the monitor's worst wake delay.
-  The rules below compare live values to the process's own policy instead of
-  copying absolute timing defaults. They remain separately pin-gated and use
-  NoData=OK so they stay inert during the coordinator restart and a rollback
-  to the pre-#214 image.
-  Coordinator restart evidence (2026-09-08, #1056): on 2026-09-06 the union
-  coordinator went from 32 to 57 restart-policy restarts in under four hours
-  while every rule above stayed green, because each exit plus its ~55s cold
-  rebuild fit inside the three-minute coordinator-scrape NoData windows. The
-  PrismCoordinator* rules read a host-side docker-inspect textfile from the
-  PRISM host's node exporter instead of the coordinator scrape, so the
-  evidence outlives the process. Coverage is one non-paging Slack rule with
-  NoData=Alerting and a ten-minute pending window, so collector-versus-rule
-  deploy order cannot page. The restart rules use increase() on a counter the
-  collector seeds at 0 and publishes only from persisted state: a lost state
-  file re-seeds downward to 0, which increase() treats as a reset, while
-  extrapolation can only round an integer count up, so ">= N" never
-  under-counts (negligible at the 15s scrape cadence). Deploys and lane
-  restarts are manual Docker starts
-  that never increment RestartCount, so planned work needs no maintenance
-  flag; drills that crash the coordinator on purpose use a Grafana silence.
